@startuml graph_states

title Graph State Machine

kSuccess : the requested Run Target is active
kInTransition : Run Target transition is in progress
kAborting : Wait for ongoing jobs to finish after\na failure or transition cancellation
kUndefinedState : No ongoing jobs, graph is in\nan undefined but consistent state

[*] --> kSuccess : construction

' --- starting a transition ---------------------------------------------------
kSuccess --> kInTransition : main loop iteration\n[a pending Run Target has been set,\ndiffers from active]\n\nstart initial transition\n\nstart transition to Off
kUndefinedState --> kInTransition : main loop iteration\n[a pending Run Target has been set]\n\nmain loop iteration\n[no pending Run Target]\nself-heal to fallback

' --- running a transition ----------------------------------------------------
kInTransition --> kInTransition : job finished\n[transition has work left]
kInTransition --> kSuccess : Last job finished\n[transition complete]

' --- leaving a transition ----------------------------------------------------
kInTransition --> kAborting : activation failed, unexpected termination,\nnew Run Target requested, supervision failure or shutdown\n[jobs still running]
kInTransition --> kUndefinedState : same as transition to kAborting\nbut with no jobs running

' --- aborting ----------------------------------------------------------------
kAborting --> kAborting : job finished\n[not the last job]
kAborting --> kUndefinedState : job finished\n[last job]
kAborting --> kAborting : cancel or new request

' --- failures outside a transition -------------------------------------------
kSuccess --> kUndefinedState : unexpected termination\n\nlate completion\n[last outstanding job]
kUndefinedState --> kUndefinedState : unexpected termination

legend bottom
  A Run Target request will record the "pending Run Target".
  The cyclic main loop will check in each iteration for a recorded pending Run Target
  and initiates the Run Target transition when in kSuccess or kUndefinedState state.

  kUndefinedState is not terminal. Every main loop iteration leaves it, towards a
  recorded pending Run Target request if there is one and towards the fallback Run 
  Target otherwise.

  Before a new transition can be started any currently ongoing jobs need to finish.
  kAborting is the state that is waiting for jobs to finish. No new jobs will be started
  in this state. When the in-flight job count reaches zero (no more jobs in process),
  the graph moves to kUndefinedState.
  
  Requests made while in kAborting do not change the state immediately, but only change the
  target for the transition that will be started in kUndefinedState.
end legend

@enduml
